WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:06.680
An OTDR, an optical time-domain reflectometer,
is a tool that is used to find faults in fiber-optic

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.450
cables, specifically kinks and breaks in the
cable.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.680
Here's how it works: A signal is sent down
the cable.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.679
If the signal goes through, the cable can
handle a signal just fine.

00:00:17.679 --> 00:00:22.029
If there is a break, the signal will bounce
off the break and back to its origin.

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:26.999
OTDRs can also measure cable length and overall
attenuation, which is its loss of signal over

00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:27.999
distance.

00:00:27.999 --> 00:00:33.430
An OTDR tool performs these actions by reading
the optical fiber, also known as a light meter.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:39.110
The copper cable equivalent of an OTDR is
simply a TDR, a time-domain reflectometer.

